From e81e5800713309a5444ae234760732a7d7125e1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:30:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 191/192] GX: three vertices left over in a quad draw draw a
 triangle, as on the hardware

Wind Waker draws each of its night stars (dKyr_drawStar) as GX_QUADS with
three vertices. The quads were taken four vertices at a time and the rest
ignored, so the stars' draws were dropped as incomplete quads and the night
sky had none. Three left over now draw a triangle; one or two still draw
nothing. Windfall's dock at 4:00 has its stars, as in Wind Waker HD.

The rule that left them out said it matched Aurora's own quad loop, but
that loop runs while v < count and emits 0,1,2 for three vertices (and a
second triangle reading past the draw), so Aurora drew the stars; the
frontend that replaced it dropped them. This draws the one real triangle.
The gxcore, frontend and replay digest tests asserted the old rule on a
three-vertex quad fixture; they now expect its triangle (three indices),
and a two-vertex quad keeps the draws-nothing case.

Smooth Motion keeps the stars steady. Each star is drawn at the camera's eye
plus its place on a dome 300 units around it, so the stars go with the
camera. The in-between frames paired them by their centres in the world:
a star the camera carried more than a few units found none, or its neighbour,
and the in-between frames drew them jumping with the camera or half way to
another star (sailing at night, 5 to 23% of an in-between frame's stars
stood where a real frame's did, and there were half as many again). A quad
of three vertices, which only the stars draw, is now paired by its centre
from the camera's eye, and its in-between frames draw it where it is now,
moved as far as each in-between camera's eye is from this frame's: 87 to 100%
of their stars stand where the real frames' do, and as many. The
frame_interp tests pass.
